Guided Exercise: Application Health Probes
Configure health probes in a deployment and verify that network clients are insulated from application failures.
Outcomes
Assess potential issues with an application that is not configured with health probes.
Configure startup, liveness, and readiness probes for the application.

1. Lab Setup
Start the lab environment:
lab start reliability-probes
What it does:
•	Creates the reliability-probes project in OpenShift.
•	Provides the long-load container image and YAML resources for this exercise.
•	This container has endpoints to simulate crashes and temporary unavailability.
Log in and select the project:
oc login -u developer -p developer https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
oc project reliability-probes
________________________________________
2. Deploy the Application Without Probes
Apply the deployment YAML:
oc apply -f ~/DO180/labs/reliability-probes/long-load-deploy.yaml
Test if the app is responding:
oc exec deploy/long-load -- curl -s localhost:3000/health
# app is still starting
Check pod readiness:
oc get pods
Observation/Theory:
•	Pods show READY 1/1 even though the application isn’t ready.
•	Without probes, Kubernetes assumes pods are ready immediately, so clients might receive requests before the app is functional.
________________________________________
3. Add a Startup Probe
Modify the deployment YAML to add:
startupProbe:
  failureThreshold: 30
  periodSeconds: 3
  httpGet:
    path: /health
    port: 3000
Apply the updated deployment:
oc apply -f ~/DO180/labs/reliability-probes/long-load-deploy.yaml
Check pod readiness:
watch oc get pods
Observation/Theory:
•	Pods now remain not ready until the startup probe succeeds.
•	Startup probes ensure the pod isn’t marked ready until the app has fully started.
•	Useful for apps with long startup times.
________________________________________
4. Add a Liveness Probe
•	Start the load test to simulate traffic:
~/DO180/labs/reliability-probes/load-test.sh
•	Make a pod unhealthy using the /togglesick endpoint:
oc exec deploy/long-load -- curl -s localhost:3000/togglesick
Modify deployment YAML to add a liveness probe:
livenessProbe:
  failureThreshold: 3
  periodSeconds: 3
  httpGet:
    path: /health
    port: 3000
Apply the updated deployment:
oc apply -f ~/DO180/labs/reliability-probes/long-load-deploy.yaml
Observation/Theory:
•	Liveness probes detect broken pods and trigger a restart.
•	In the load test, unhealthy pod is restarted automatically, while healthy pods continue serving requests.
•	Liveness probes help maintain high availability by ensuring broken pods don’t serve traffic.
________________________________________
5. Add a Readiness Probe
•	Scale down deployment to 0 replicas:
oc scale deploy/long-load --replicas 0
•	Add readiness probe via CLI:
oc set probe deploy/long-load \
  --readiness --failure-threshold 1 --period-seconds 3 \
  --get-url http://:3000/health
•	Scale deployment back up:
oc scale deploy/long-load --replicas 3
watch oc get pods
•	Simulate temporary unavailability with /hiccup:
oc exec deploy/long-load -- curl -s localhost:3000/hiccup?time=5
Observation/Theory:
•	Pod is marked not ready during temporary failure (5 seconds).
•	Readiness probe ensures traffic is only sent to healthy pods, insulating clients from temporary issues.
•	Unlike liveness probe, the pod is not restarted because the issue is temporary.
________________________________________
6. Key Takeaways (Theory)
Probe Type	Purpose	Behavior
Startup Probe	Detect when pod is fully started	Runs until success; ignores afterwards; prevents early readiness
Readiness Probe	Determines if pod can receive traffic	Removes pod from service endpoints when unhealthy; adds back when healthy
Liveness Probe	Checks pod is alive	Restarts pod if unhealthy; runs continuously
Best Practice:
•	Use startup probe for long start times.
•	Use readiness probe to protect clients from traffic to unhealthy pods.
•	Use liveness probe to restart broken pods.
